WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.480 --> 00:00:05.280
Superman might be the world's most famous&nbsp;
superhero, but Clark Kent is pretty impressive&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:05.280 --> 00:00:10.640
in his own right. Still, there's a whole lot even&nbsp;
the most diehard fans get wrong about Superman's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:10.640 --> 00:00:15.920
mild-mannered alter-ego. Here are a few of&nbsp;
the biggest misconceptions about Clark Kent.

00:00:16.240 --> 00:00:19.200
When it comes to pulling off&nbsp;
incredible feats of heroism,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:19.200 --> 00:00:25.360
people tend to go with the invincible alien&nbsp;
Superman over the demure reporter Clark Kent.

00:00:25.360 --> 00:00:27.680
"I'm here to fight for truth,&nbsp;
justice, and the American way."

00:00:28.640 --> 00:00:33.600
As such, you might think that Kent isn't all that&nbsp;
when he's not wearing the red-and-blue spandex.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:33.600 --> 00:00:37.920
But the truth is, while Kent's second&nbsp;
life as a superhero tends to overshadow&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:37.920 --> 00:00:41.840
his journalistic accomplishments, that&nbsp;
doesn't make them any less impressive.

00:00:41.840 --> 00:00:45.440
His byline frequently appears on&nbsp;
the front page of The Daily Planet,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:45.440 --> 00:00:50.640
arguably the most widely-read newspaper in the&nbsp;
DC universe's version of the United States.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:50.640 --> 00:00:54.800
And while there aren't many comics&nbsp;
about Kent conducting lengthy interviews&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:54.800 --> 00:00:58.000
and typing at his computer, direct&nbsp;
evidence of his award-winning track&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:58.000 --> 00:01:02.800
record appears in the legendary Mark&nbsp;
Waid and Alex Ross comic, Kingdom Come.

00:01:02.800 --> 00:01:07.200
The scene in question starts with Kent —&nbsp;
who's going by his Kryptonian birth name,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:07.200 --> 00:01:12.240
Kal-El — chatting with Wonder Woman outside the&nbsp;
Watchtower. Kal-El is halfway through recalling&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:12.240 --> 00:01:17.360
a recent final encounter with Brainiac when he&nbsp;
mentions that some of the villain's circuitry has&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:17.360 --> 00:01:24.000
been hidden inside a Pulitzer in Clark Kent's&nbsp;
apartment. Take note: he says a Pulitzer, not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:24.000 --> 00:01:28.640
the Pulitzer, suggesting that, incredibly,&nbsp;
Kent might actually have more than one.

00:01:29.120 --> 00:01:33.360
One of Superman's constants throughout the&nbsp;
character's 80-plus years of history is the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:33.360 --> 00:01:38.320
tension between his small town upbringing and&nbsp;
his adult life in the fast-paced metropolis&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:38.320 --> 00:01:43.200
of, uh, Metropolis. As such, whenever&nbsp;
someone accuses the concept of Superman&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:43.200 --> 00:01:48.720
of being old-fashioned, that someone is usually&nbsp;
talking about Clark Kent's midwestern identity,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:48.720 --> 00:01:54.080
rather than anything specific to Superman as a&nbsp;
hero. Indeed, the wholesome, Norman Rockwell-esque&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:54.080 --> 00:01:59.600
image of the American bread basket that underlies&nbsp;
Smallville does scan as a little quaint.

00:01:59.600 --> 00:02:03.440
But the Clark Kent character isn't as&nbsp;
old-fashioned as you might assume. Take&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:03.440 --> 00:02:08.400
one of the other constants in Superman's life:&nbsp;
the romance between him and Lois Lane. If Kent's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:08.400 --> 00:02:13.840
really such a slack-jawed hayseed, then how could&nbsp;
a sophisticated, cynical journalist like Lois&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:13.840 --> 00:02:18.960
be in love with him? It's also worth considering&nbsp;
that traveling to unfamiliar countries tends to be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:18.960 --> 00:02:24.240
enough to broaden a person's horizons, and Kent's&nbsp;
been to a whole bunch of different planets — which&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:24.240 --> 00:02:29.520
would suggest that his horizons are plenty broad,&nbsp;
no matter how simple his upbringing may have been.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:29.520 --> 00:02:34.240
So while it may be totally fair to call Clark&nbsp;
Kent square in the Huey Lewis sense of the word,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:34.240 --> 00:02:38.240
don't go thinking he's a man of the past&nbsp;
— because the truth is he's anything but.

00:02:38.800 --> 00:02:43.440
Clark Kent's need to keep his superhero identity&nbsp;
hidden from the world is one of the most important&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:43.440 --> 00:02:48.000
aspects of the character — and he's gone&nbsp;
to some pretty crazy lengths to do it, too.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:48.000 --> 00:02:52.320
So it might be easy to imagine that the world&nbsp;
will never really know Superman's secret. But&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:52.320 --> 00:02:59.200
this all changed in February 2020 with the release&nbsp;
of Superman Vol. 5 #18 . In this story, Superman&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:59.200 --> 00:03:03.680
gets burnt out on keeping secrets, and finally&nbsp;
lets the world know the truth: that he's actually&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:03.680 --> 00:03:08.560
been the Daily Planet reporter and Smallville&nbsp;
native Clark Kent this whole friggin' time.

00:03:08.560 --> 00:03:13.840
Of course, the status quo is never static for long&nbsp;
in the world of DC Comics, so it's impossible to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:13.840 --> 00:03:18.800
know how long Superman's identity will remain&nbsp;
public knowledge. But until the next continuity&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:18.800 --> 00:03:23.920
reset or massive retcon changes everything&nbsp;
back again, the heroes and citizens of the DC&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:23.920 --> 00:03:28.640
universe will continue to know that Clark Kent&nbsp;
is really Superman…. For better or for worse.

00:03:29.120 --> 00:03:34.320
Plenty of people wrongly assume that Batman and&nbsp;
Superman are natural adversaries — and the blame&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:34.320 --> 00:03:39.520
for this misconception doesn't fall entirely on&nbsp;
Zack Snyder's shoulders, either. While Batman v&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:39.520 --> 00:03:44.400
Superman certainly popularized the notion that&nbsp;
the two characters hate each other, the pop&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:44.400 --> 00:03:49.200
culture commentator Chuck Klosterman described&nbsp;
their relationship as that of arch-enemies&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:49.200 --> 00:03:54.640
in a 2007 essay, almost a full decade before the&nbsp;
release of Snyder's film. On top of that, the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:54.640 --> 00:03:59.920
comics themselves have occasionally pitted the two&nbsp;
against each other, in a wide variety of contexts.

00:03:59.920 --> 00:04:04.080
And while it's true that Clark Kent and Bruce&nbsp;
Wayne have a very complicated relationship,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:04.080 --> 00:04:08.160
you only need to take a cursory glance&nbsp;
at some more recent Superman stories&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:08.160 --> 00:04:12.720
to know that the friendship between these two&nbsp;
heroes plays a crucial role in the DC universe.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:12.720 --> 00:04:16.800
A recent run of Batman even commemorated&nbsp;
their bond with a two-part arc, in which&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:16.800 --> 00:04:22.160
each hero agonizes over the other being far more&nbsp;
noble, brave, and kind-hearted than themselves.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:22.160 --> 00:04:26.000
They then promptly get over it, go on&nbsp;
a double date with Lois and Selina,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:26.000 --> 00:04:30.160
and visit a batting cage, where they&nbsp;
argue over whether Bruce can land a hit&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:30.160 --> 00:04:34.240
on Clark's Kryptonian fastball. See?&nbsp;
They're practically best buddies.

00:04:34.720 --> 00:04:40.320
So here's a question: just how close are Clark&nbsp;
Kent and Bruce Wayne? Well, turns out they're such&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:40.320 --> 00:04:46.560
pals that they've even traded outfits on a handful&nbsp;
of occasions. That's right: Clark Kent is Batman!

00:04:46.560 --> 00:04:52.000
Well, sometimes. In Tom King and Clay Mann's&nbsp;
run on Batman, Bruce Wayne dresses up as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:52.000 --> 00:04:56.080
Superman and Clark Kent dons the cape&nbsp;
and cowl. This is pretty much their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:56.080 --> 00:04:59.600
only option in the moment, as they're&nbsp;
trying to meet the costume criteria for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:59.600 --> 00:05:03.760
superhero night at the Gotham County Fair&nbsp;
without revealing their secret identities.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:03.760 --> 00:05:08.480
A glasses-wearing Kent-Batman is exactly&nbsp;
as funny as it sounds too, especially when&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:08.480 --> 00:05:13.360
Lois Lane has to talk him out of using his&nbsp;
super-strength to win her a carnival prize.

00:05:13.360 --> 00:05:19.040
In another instance, mind-controlling nanites take&nbsp;
Bruce Wayne out of action in a 1998 episode of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:19.040 --> 00:05:23.840
Superman: The Animated Series, leading Clark Kent&nbsp;
to temporarily take up the mantle of the Batman.

00:05:28.320 --> 00:05:30.320
"Right here. Look familiar?"

00:05:30.320 --> 00:05:30.820
"Bane…"

00:05:31.360 --> 00:05:33.200
"Ah, yes, Bane."

00:05:33.760 --> 00:05:37.600
Kent and Tim Drake eventually rescue their&nbsp;
brooding colleague, and in the process,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:37.600 --> 00:05:42.160
prove something long suspected of Superman:&nbsp;
That, with the possible exception of the Joker,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:42.160 --> 00:05:46.240
he can brush aside the bulk of Batman's&nbsp;
rogues gallery without much effort.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:46.240 --> 00:05:50.880
Can't see Batman dispatching Doomsday so&nbsp;
easily, can you? Time to up the ante, Bruce.

00:05:51.440 --> 00:05:55.520
Ask someone the most important difference&nbsp;
between Clark Kent and Superman, and they'll&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:55.520 --> 00:06:00.080
probably tell you it's the fact that Clark Kent&nbsp;
wears glasses. It's also pretty well known that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:00.080 --> 00:06:05.600
this flimsy little disguise is enough to fool&nbsp;
everybody Kal-El meets. But some people have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:05.600 --> 00:06:10.160
seen through the disguise — such as one&nbsp;
particularly sly resident of Gotham city.

00:06:10.160 --> 00:06:14.880
During Bruce Wayne and Selina Kyle's engagement,&nbsp;
Kent expresses some reservations about meeting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:14.880 --> 00:06:19.680
his buddy's new fiancé. After all, revealing&nbsp;
his secret identity to a former super-criminal&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:19.680 --> 00:06:24.720
seems like a pretty risky bet. But Lois lets her&nbsp;
husband know that Selina seems naturally gifted&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:24.720 --> 00:06:29.440
at figuring out people's secrets, and that the&nbsp;
chances are she already knows who Kent really is.

00:06:29.440 --> 00:06:34.160
The very next page finds Batman and Catwoman&nbsp;
watching over the Gotham City skyline.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:34.160 --> 00:06:38.720
The feline fatale quickly confirms Lane's&nbsp;
suspicions, wryly pointing out that a little&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:38.720 --> 00:06:43.280
hair gel and a pair of glasses isn't quite the&nbsp;
foolproof disguise that Clark Kent thinks it is.

00:06:43.840 --> 00:06:48.080
Though Catwoman is far too insightful to&nbsp;
fall for Clark Kent's silly glasses routine,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:48.080 --> 00:06:52.000
it's worth pointing out that pretty much&nbsp;
everyone else does. In Grant Morrison and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:52.000 --> 00:06:56.560
Frank Quietly's All-Star Superman, for example,&nbsp;
even those nearest and dearest to Kent can't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:56.560 --> 00:07:01.520
seem to put two and two together. Lex Luthor,&nbsp;
allegedly the smartest man in the whole world,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:01.520 --> 00:07:06.000
looks a glasses-less Kent right in the&nbsp;
face and makes no connection whatsoever.

00:07:06.000 --> 00:07:10.800
In another scene, the staff of the Daily Planet&nbsp;
watches as Kent stares down a powered-up Luthor.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:10.800 --> 00:07:14.640
When it becomes obvious that this is&nbsp;
actually Superman confronting his nemesis,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:14.640 --> 00:07:19.280
Jimmy Olsen simply suggests that Superman&nbsp;
has disguised himself as Clark Kent.

00:07:19.280 --> 00:07:22.800
Of course, you could argue that it's&nbsp;
hard to recognize a god-like superhero&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:22.800 --> 00:07:26.320
when he's standing next to you at the&nbsp;
water cooler, and some people do look&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:26.320 --> 00:07:30.000
totally different when they're wearing&nbsp;
or not wearing a pair of glasses. Still,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:30.000 --> 00:07:33.760
though, you'd think that some of the world's&nbsp;
greatest reporters might have caught on by now.

00:07:34.240 --> 00:07:38.080
Frank Miller's The Dark Knight Returns&nbsp;
might be a pretty great comic, but it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:38.080 --> 00:07:43.040
was the worst thing to ever happen to Clark&nbsp;
Kent's reputation. This iconic comic book&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:43.040 --> 00:07:48.240
story sees a Ronald Reagan-esque United States&nbsp;
president order Superman to bring in Batman,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:48.240 --> 00:07:52.880
whose tactics don't fall in step with the&nbsp;
government's new anti-vigilante regulations.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:52.880 --> 00:07:58.720
Batman quite rightly accuses his Justice League&nbsp;
co-founder of spinelessness in the face of… well,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:58.720 --> 00:08:04.560
basically anyone with a badge or a flag. Thus, one&nbsp;
of the most famous superhero stories of all time&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:04.560 --> 00:08:09.520
successfully makes the point that the most&nbsp;
famous superhero of all time is kind of a chump.

00:08:09.520 --> 00:08:13.440
But while The Dark Knight Returns&nbsp;
portrayed Kent as deferential and servile,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:13.440 --> 00:08:18.160
he hasn't always been the kind of guy who has to&nbsp;
do whatever authority figures tell him. In fact,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:18.160 --> 00:08:22.800
Superman has brought down President Lex Luthor on&nbsp;
a few different occasions, and in one instance,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:22.800 --> 00:08:28.400
he even goes full Man of Steel and outright&nbsp;
fries Luthor's democratically-elected brains.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:28.400 --> 00:08:33.760
And then there's Justice League Unlimited: The&nbsp;
macro-plot of this 2004 animated series revolves&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:33.760 --> 00:08:38.480
around Superman refusing to play ball with&nbsp;
high-ranking government employee Amanda Waller.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:38.480 --> 00:08:42.320
So don't go thinking Kent doesn't know&nbsp;
how to play the rebel when it suits him.

00:08:42.320 --> 00:08:47.360
Still, you've got to hand it to mid-1980s Frank&nbsp;
Miller: Only he could convince the world that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:47.360 --> 00:08:52.160
Superman would attack Batman because the President&nbsp;
thought Batman was fighting crime too hard.

00:08:52.560 --> 00:08:56.560
Clark Kent is the original Superman&nbsp;
and the default Superman, and no one's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:56.560 --> 00:09:01.040
ever gonna make the case to the contrary.&nbsp;
But he's not the only Superman. In fact,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:01.040 --> 00:09:05.920
during DC's line-wide Future State&nbsp;
event, Clark Kent isn't Superman at all.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:05.920 --> 00:09:10.400
Instead, that designation belongs to&nbsp;
Jonathan Kent, Clark's son with Lois Lane.

00:09:10.400 --> 00:09:15.200
Overall, though, Clark has had an easier time&nbsp;
hanging onto the exclusive use of his famous&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:15.200 --> 00:09:19.360
alias than some of the other heroes out&nbsp;
there. But let's not forget the brief&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:19.360 --> 00:09:24.480
period following Clark's "death" in the '90s,&nbsp;
in which Steel, Hank Henshaw, Conner Kent,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:24.480 --> 00:09:28.560
and the Eradicator were all briefly&nbsp;
and unofficially considered "Superman."&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:28.560 --> 00:09:32.560
In one of the most memorable chapters of&nbsp;
Superman: The Animated Series, Bizarro&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:32.560 --> 00:09:37.360
also successfully passes himself off as&nbsp;
the genuine article... for about a day.

00:09:37.360 --> 00:09:46.080
"How? How could he? I'm Superman! Me… Me!"

00:09:46.720 --> 00:09:51.600
Still, nobody apart from Clark seems able&nbsp;
to hold down the gig for very long. So does&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:51.600 --> 00:09:55.760
this mean that being Superman is more&nbsp;
difficult than, say, being Spider-Man?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:55.760 --> 00:09:59.440
Well, who could say? But a whole lot of&nbsp;
people have been Spider-Man over the years,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:59.440 --> 00:10:04.320
and only one man seems able to pull off being&nbsp;
Superman. Something to consider, don't you think?

00:10:04.880 --> 00:10:08.400
To most people, the names "Superman"&nbsp;
and "Lois Lane" seem like natural&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:08.400 --> 00:10:13.520
bedfellows. The ABC series Lois &amp; Clark only&nbsp;
strengthened that connection in the '90s,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:13.520 --> 00:10:17.840
and 2021's CW series Superman &amp;&nbsp;
Lois is likely to do the same.

00:10:17.840 --> 00:10:21.520
But Clark Kent and Lois Lane haven't&nbsp;
been romantically involved for the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:21.520 --> 00:10:25.760
entirety of their respective fictional&nbsp;
existences. As many readers may be aware,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:25.760 --> 00:10:31.280
Clark's high school sweetheart and ongoing&nbsp;
semi-platonic gal pal is actually Lana Lang.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:31.280 --> 00:10:34.560
Sporadically throughout the last&nbsp;
roughly 40 years worth of comics,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:34.560 --> 00:10:39.440
Superman has also gotten romantically tangled&nbsp;
up with Princess Diana of Themyscira .

00:10:39.440 --> 00:10:44.480
In fact, Superman and Wonder Woman have far more&nbsp;
in common than Lois and Clark — or Diana and Steve&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:44.480 --> 00:10:49.360
Trevor, for that matter. But every time these two&nbsp;
heroes get together, sooner or later, they usually&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:49.360 --> 00:10:54.000
end up back with their traditional partners.&nbsp;
Maybe that's just because those characters need to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:54.000 --> 00:10:59.520
periodically reset to their status quos. Or maybe&nbsp;
it's because Wonder Woman and Superman always get&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:59.520 --> 00:11:04.560
to know each other as superheroes, and neither can&nbsp;
live up to those hyper-idyllic first impressions.

00:11:04.560 --> 00:11:07.600
But Lois? Well, she first meets him as Clark Kent,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:07.600 --> 00:11:11.520
a regular ol' joe from Kansas — so her&nbsp;
expectations for their relationship are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:11.520 --> 00:11:15.200
just a little more grounded. Probably&nbsp;
makes for a simpler date night, too.

